        "boundariesFromNativeProseFlow": [
          {
            "index": 1,
            "before": "Light falling on a surface is usually distributed unevenly.\nFor example, a lamp suspended above a table often illuminates the center most strongly, while the edges are noticeably dimmer.\nEven for a point source, the center receives more luminous flux than the edges, so the difference is not caused solely by the lamp emitting different luminous intensities in different directions.",
            "after": "The ratio of the luminous flux $\\Phi$ falling on a surface to its area $S$ is called the illuminance $E$.\n$E=\\frac{\\Phi}{S}$ (6.5)"
          },
          {
            "index": 5,
            "before": "With all other conditions unchanged, illuminance is proportional to the source's luminous intensity.\nTwo identical sources at the same position emit twice as much light energy in a given direction per unit time as one source.\nThey can therefore be replaced by a single source with twice the luminous intensity.",
            "after": "Place a point source at the center of a spherical surface to examine how illuminance depends on distance.\nThe sphere has surface area $S=4\\pi R^2$, and the total luminous flux is $\\Phi=4\\pi I$ (see equation 6.4).\nThe illuminance on the sphere is therefore:\n$E=\\frac{\\Phi}{S}=\\frac{4\\pi I}{4\\pi R^2}=\\frac{I}{R^2}$"
          },
          {
            "index": 7,
            "before": "Thus, the illuminance of the surface is inversely proportional to the square of its distance from the source.",
            "after": "In the spherical example above, the rays strike the surface perpendicularly.\nLight does not always arrive perpendicularly; its direction relative to a surface is described by the angle of incidence."
          },
          {
            "index": 22,
            "before": "With several light sources, the total illuminance is the sum of the illuminances produced by the individual sources.",
            "after": "Illuminance can be measured with a specialized instrument, called a lumen meter (illuminance meter) in the source textbook.\nPhotographers also use exposure meters to determine exposure times on the basis of illuminance measurements."
          }
        ],
